@namespace PgmStudio.Client.Components

@* One terrain-paint material, edited as a form (docs/world-export/terrain-painting.md §3). A material is
   recursive — a pattern's palette entry, a tint's neutral fallback, a stack's layer are themselves materials —
   so this component nests inside itself. It edits the theme JSON node in place, which IS the wire format the
   painter deserializes, so there is no second model of a material to drift from it. *@

<div class="material-editor @(Nested ? "material-editor--nested" : null)">
  @* What the entry is called heads its block rather than sharing the row with the controls: inline it was
     one more thing competing for the row's width, and it pushed the kind select out of line with every
     other kind select in the stack. *@
  @if (Label is not null) { <span class="material-editor-label">@Label</span> }

  @* One row of controls: what kind it is, how far it reaches, and the way to remove it. The extent belongs
     to the list that owns the entry rather than to the material, so it arrives as HeadExtra — and it is a
     CoordField, which carries its unit inside the box and so keeps the row one control tall. A labelled
     Field here stacked a label above its input, which left the number sitting lower than the select. *@
  <div class="material-editor-head">
    <select class="field-input field-input--slim" value="@Kind" @onchange="ChangeKind">
      @foreach (var kind in MaterialKind.All)
      {
        <option value="@kind.Id" @key="kind.Id">@kind.Name</option>
      }
    </select>
    @* What a kind is belongs beside the control that names it, and only where the material itself is being
       authored. A nested layer, band or stop is a reuse of a kind, not a second definition of it — a note
       on each one restated the same paragraph as many times as the stack was deep, which is what made a
       three-layer voronoi stack unreadable. *@
    @if (!Nested)
    {
      <HelpMark Open="helpOpen" Title="@($"What a {MaterialKind.NameOf(Kind).ToLowerInvariant()} is")"
                OnClick="() => helpOpen = !helpOpen" />
    }
    @HeadExtra
    @if (OnRemove.HasDelegate)
    {
      <Button Variant="icon" Icon="trash-2" Title="Remove" OnClick="OnRemove" />
    }
  </div>

  @if (!Nested && helpOpen)
  {
    <div class="help-note-body">
      @switch (Kind)
      {
        case MaterialKind.Solid:
          <p>One block, everywhere this material paints. The picker is the export's own palette, so the
            swatch is the colour that will land.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.TeamTint:
          <p>Takes the owning team's colour on the same 0–15 scale as wool, so the block has to be one that
            colours by damage. Land with no team falls through to the neutral material.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Layered:
          <p>A stack claimed downward from the top of the band — grass over two dirt. The last layer repeats
            past the bottom of the stack, so a deeper band never falls through.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Voronoi:
          <p>Cells roughly one patch-size across, drawn as a <strong>ramp inward from the cell
            boundary</strong>. The first band sits on the boundary itself, so it draws the grid; each one
            after it is a ring further in, and the last takes the middle. A cell too small to reach a band
            never shows it, so small cells come out filled by whichever band they did reach. Deterministic —
            the same seed always paints it.</p>
          <p>@RiseNote</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Cell:
          <p>Patches of one material each, any two of which may meet — the fabric a voronoi's diagram is not.
            <strong>Jitter</strong> is how far a patch's seed strays from its grid square (0% is the grid
            itself, 100% is shards); <strong>edge wander</strong> is how many blocks the boundaries wobble,
            which is what makes the patches organic rather than straight-edged.</p>
          <p>@RiseNote</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Noise:
        case MaterialKind.Turbulence:
        case MaterialKind.Electric:
          <p>@FieldBlurb Cut into bands by the stops in order — the first takes the lowest slice of the
            field. Only neighbouring stops ever share a boundary, and the outermost two are accents rather
            than halves, so a stop list reads as a ramp from one material to the next. More octaves add finer
            detail without narrowing the field.</p>
          <p>@RiseNote</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.WallRun:
          <p>Stripes running along the wall that wrap the whole void-facing perimeter, corners included.
            Widths are in wall cells and the stripes repeat in order.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.WallDiagonal:
          <p>The same stripes, sheared: each course starts <strong>slope</strong> cells further round the
            wall than the one below it, so they lean instead of standing. A slope of 1 moves one cell along
            per course up, which is 45°; larger lays them flatter and a negative slope leans them the other
            way. The shear is read off world height, so two walls of different heights standing together
            meet with their diagonals in line.</p>
          break;

        case MaterialKind.Checker:
          <p>Two materials alternating over squares of the given side. The board is laid <strong>in the face
            it paints</strong> — along the wall and up it on an outer wall, across the ground everywhere
            else — so it reads as squares on both rather than as stripes down a wall.</p>
          break;
      }
    </div>
  }

  <div class="material-editor-body">
    @switch (Kind)
    {
      case MaterialKind.Solid:
        <BlockPicker Blocks="Blocks" Id="@Int(ThemeFields.Id, 1)" Data="@Int(ThemeFields.Data, 0)" OnPick="PickSolid" />
        break;

      case MaterialKind.TeamTint:
        <BlockPicker Blocks="Blocks" Id="@Int(ThemeFields.BlockId, 159)" Data="0" ShowColors="false" OnPick="PickTintBlock" />
        <MaterialEditor Nested Label="Neutral land" Blocks="Blocks" Node="Neutral" OnChanged="Changed" />
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Layered:
        @foreach (var layer in List(ThemeFields.Layers))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Label="@($"Layer {layer.Index + 1}")" Blocks="Blocks" @key="layer.Node"
                          Node="@layer.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@layer.Remove">
            <HeadExtra>
              <CoordField Prefix="Courses" Type="number" Value="@layer.Extent" OnChange="@layer.SetExtent"
                          min="1" step="1" title="How many courses this layer claims" />
            </HeadExtra>
          </MaterialEditor>
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Layers, "Add layer")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Voronoi:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Patch size">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.CellSize, 10)" @onchange="SetCellSize" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Rise">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   title="Vertical period in blocks. 0 keeps the pattern flat."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Rise, 0)" @onchange="SetRise" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Seed">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Seed, 0)" @onchange="SetSeed" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @foreach (var band in List(ThemeFields.Bands))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Label="@BandLabel(band.Index)" Blocks="Blocks" @key="band.Node"
                          Node="@band.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@band.Remove">
            <HeadExtra>
              <CoordField Prefix="Blocks" Type="number" Value="@band.Extent" OnChange="@band.SetExtent"
                          min="1" step="1" title="How many blocks in from the cell boundary this band runs" />
            </HeadExtra>
          </MaterialEditor>
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Bands, "Add a band")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Cell:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Patch size">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.CellSize, 10)" @onchange="SetCellSize" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Jitter %">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" max="100" step="5"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Jitter, 50)" @onchange="SetJitter" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Edge wander">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Warp, 4)" @onchange="SetWarp" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Rise">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   title="Vertical period in blocks. 0 keeps the pattern flat."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Rise, 0)" @onchange="SetRise" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Seed">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Seed, 0)" @onchange="SetSeed" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @foreach (var entry in List(ThemeFields.Palette))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Label="@($"Patch {entry.Index + 1}")" Blocks="Blocks" @key="entry.Node"
                          Node="@entry.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@entry.Remove" />
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Palette, "Add a patch material")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Noise:
      case MaterialKind.Turbulence:
      case MaterialKind.Electric:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Scale">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Scale, 16)" @onchange="SetScale" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Octaves">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" max="8" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Octaves, 3)" @onchange="SetOctaves" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Rise">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   title="Vertical period in blocks. 0 keeps the pattern flat."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Rise, 0)" @onchange="SetRise" />
          </Field>
          <Field Label="Seed">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="0" step="1"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Seed, 0)" @onchange="SetSeed" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @foreach (var entry in List(ThemeFields.Stops))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Label="@($"Stop {entry.Index + 1}")" Blocks="Blocks" @key="entry.Node"
                          Node="@entry.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@entry.Remove" />
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Stops, "Add stop")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.WallRun:
        @foreach (var run in List(ThemeFields.Runs))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Label="@($"Stripe {run.Index + 1}")" Blocks="Blocks" @key="run.Node"
                          Node="@run.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@run.Remove">
            <HeadExtra>
              <CoordField Prefix="Cells" Type="number" Value="@run.Extent" OnChange="@run.SetExtent"
                          min="1" step="1" title="How many wall cells wide this stripe runs" />
            </HeadExtra>
          </MaterialEditor>
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Runs, "Add stripe")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.WallDiagonal:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Slope">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" step="1"
                   title="Cells along the wall per course up. 1 is 45°, 0 is a vertical run."
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Slope, 1)" @onchange="SetSlope" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        @foreach (var run in List(ThemeFields.Runs))
        {
          <MaterialEditor Nested Label="@($"Stripe {run.Index + 1}")" Blocks="Blocks" @key="run.Node"
                          Node="@run.Material" OnChanged="Changed" OnRemove="@run.Remove">
            <HeadExtra>
              <CoordField Prefix="Cells" Type="number" Value="@run.Extent" OnChange="@run.SetExtent"
                          min="1" step="1" title="How many wall cells wide this stripe runs" />
            </HeadExtra>
          </MaterialEditor>
        }
        @ListFoot(ThemeFields.Runs, "Add stripe")
        break;

      case MaterialKind.Checker:
        <div class="material-editor-scalars">
          <Field Label="Square">
            <input class="field-input field-input--slim" type="number" min="1" step="1"
                   title="The side of one square, in blocks"
                   value="@Int(ThemeFields.Size, 1)" @onchange="SetSize" />
          </Field>
        </div>
        <MaterialEditor Nested Label="Light square" Blocks="Blocks" Node="Even" OnChanged="Changed" />
        <MaterialEditor Nested Label="Dark square" Blocks="Blocks" Node="Odd" OnChanged="Changed" />
        break;
    }
  </div>
</div>

@code {
    @* The footer under one of a material's lists: add a single entry, or fill the list from a whole family.
       One template rather than five copies — every list carries the same pair, and the field it acts on is the
       only thing that differs between them. The select shows the family the list currently holds, so it reads
       as a label until an author narrows the list and as an offer again once they have. *@
    private RenderFragment ListFoot(string field, string addLabel) => @<div class="material-editor-foot">
        <Button Icon="plus" OnClick="() => Add(field)">@addLabel</Button>
        @if (Families.Any())
        {
            <select class="field-input field-input--slim" value="@MatchedFamily(field)"
                    title="Fill this list with a whole family of blocks, then remove the ones this ground does not use"
                    @onchange="e => ApplyFamily(field, e)">
                <option value="">Fill from a family…</option>
                @foreach (var family in Families)
                {
                    <option value="@family.Key" @key="family.Key">@family.Key (@family.Count())</option>
                }
            </select>
        }
    </div>;
}
